KO-04-C4  Failure after commit, before flush
A = 64B of 0x41 ('A'), B = 64B of 0x42 ('B'), document 'doc'; fault = simulated EIO from the directory fsync
that follows the CURRENT rename (FlushFileBuffers on the directory handle here).

== Mechanism
   rename(CURRENT.tmp -> CURRENT) is the commit point: it is atomic, so every
   reader immediately sees v2. But the rename only changed the directory in
   memory; it becomes durable when the DIRECTORY is flushed (fsync(dirfd) /
   FlushFileBuffers). If that flush fails with EIO, we know neither 'committed and
   durable' nor 'not committed':
    - reporting FAILURE would be a lie if the rename is already on disk (and
      deleting v2 to 'undo' it would destroy a committed version);
    - reporting SUCCESS would be a lie if a power cut loses the rename;
    - retrying fsync is unsafe (fsyncgate, PostgreSQL 2018: after a failed fsync
      the kernel may drop the dirty state, so a second fsync 'succeeds' falsely).
   So the store raises CommitUnknownFsync, poisons the handle, deletes nothing past
   the commit point, and lets recovery read the truth back from disk on reopen.

