== Power loss after the failed flush (SimFS)
   The EIO means: the directory entry change (CURRENT -> v2) may or may not have
   reached the disk. SimFS replays the same put(B) with the same fault (the failed
   fsync emits no durability barrier) and enumerates every disk state a power cut
   at that moment could leave; the REAL recovery code reads each one.
   PASS SimFS replay: put raised CommitUnknownFsync  - CommitUnknownFsync
      k=14  write 91B .CURRENT.5068.48d09f2b.tmp
      k=15  fsync .CURRENT.5068.48d09f2b.tmp
      k=16  rename .CURRENT.5068.48d09f2b.tmp -> CURRENT
      k=16  fsync dir doc -> EIO (injected, no barrier)
   power cut after the rename, before a successful directory flush:
      3 distinct disk states -> A=2 B=1
   PASS every state recovers to exactly A or exactly B (never mixed, never empty)  - A=2 B=1
   PASS both outcomes really occur (A=2, B=1) -> the outcome IS unknown
   reopen inside the simulator: recovery rolled_forward=['doc'], serves 64B of 0x42 ('B')
   power cut DURING recovery: 7 distinct states -> A=2 B=5
   PASS power cut during recovery -> still only A or B  - A=2 B=5
   power cut AFTER recovery returned: 1 distinct states -> B=1
   PASS after recovery served B, every power-loss state is B (decision made durable)  - B=1

== Assumptions
   - the EIO is simulated (injected in the FS layer); the store code is unmodified;
   - local file system with atomic same-directory rename; drive honours flushes;
   - power loss is simulated by SimFS (ALICE model), not a real power cut.
   total time 0.5s

